\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Why the lift must be bilinear. The command $u$ reaches the tracked output through a \emph  {configuration-dependent} gain $\Phi (x)$ (a heading rotation $R(\theta )$ for a vehicle, a Jacobian $J(q)$ for an arm), so the input's effect is $\Phi (x)\,u$. A \emph  {linear} Koopman lift captures the input-free motion but must apply a single \emph  {constant} input matrix, so it cannot reproduce the state-dependent gain (\textcolor {red!75!black}{{\fontfamily  {pzd}\fontencoding  {U}\fontseries  {m}\fontshape  {n}\selectfont  \char 55}}). A \emph  {bilinear} lift instead lets the input gain $B(x)$ follow the state and matches $\Phi (x)$ (\textcolor {green!50!black}{{\fontfamily  {pzd}\fontencoding  {U}\fontseries  {m}\fontshape  {n}\selectfont  \char 51}}), at the same rollout cost; this is made precise in Prop.~\ref {prop:crit}.}}{1}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:concept}{{1}{1}{Why the lift must be bilinear. The command $u$ reaches the tracked output through a \emph {configuration-dependent} gain $\Phi (x)$ (a heading rotation $R(\theta )$ for a vehicle, a Jacobian $J(q)$ for an arm), so the input's effect is $\Phi (x)\,u$. A \emph {linear} Koopman lift captures the input-free motion but must apply a single \emph {constant} input matrix, so it cannot reproduce the state-dependent gain (\textcolor {red!75!black}{\ding {55}}). A \emph {bilinear} lift instead lets the input gain $B(x)$ follow the state and matches $\Phi (x)$ (\textcolor {green!50!black}{\ding {51}}), at the same rollout cost; this is made precise in Prop.~\ref {prop:crit}}{figure.1}{}}
