\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces The boundary case. Quadrotor open-loop prediction error vs.\ horizon: the bilinear model is $2$--$4\times $ better on the \emph  {full} state (it captures the thrust--attitude coupling), but on the control-relevant \emph  {velocity} the linear and bilinear curves coincide. Second-order, acceleration-mediated coupling is the delimited regime where a linear lift suffices---indeed is preferable.}}{7}{figure.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:quad}{{9}{7}{The boundary case. Quadrotor open-loop prediction error vs.\ horizon: the bilinear model is $2$--$4\times $ better on the \emph {full} state (it captures the thrust--attitude coupling), but on the control-relevant \emph {velocity} the linear and bilinear curves coincide. Second-order, acceleration-mediated coupling is the delimited regime where a linear lift suffices---indeed is preferable}{figure.9}{}}
